1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,533
It is quarter past one in the afternoon, on a simulated ward in India.

2
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:11,433
Ananya Rao, fifty four, with type 2 diabetes, has just eaten lunch: rice, potato and ghee.

3
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:15,996
Her glucose reads one hundred and fifty five. In range, for now.

4
00:00:16,396 --> 00:00:24,220
But her digital twin already sees where it is going: the forecast climbs well past one eighty within the hour, and an Urgent alert is on the screen.

5
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:31,023
This is GlucoTwin: a digital twin for type 2 diabetes that shows the doctor the next two hours.

6
00:00:31,423 --> 00:00:43,220
In the next twenty five minutes: the problem and why it matters for India, the data, the architecture, the modelling approach, the working dashboard, the results, scale, and the real-world impact.

7
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,442
Chapter one. The problem.

8
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:55,796
India has about one hundred and one million adults living with diabetes, and about one hundred and thirty six million more with prediabetes.

9
00:00:56,196 --> 00:01:04,110
Those are the national estimates of the ICMR-INDIAB study, in the Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology, 2023.

10
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:11,363
Among people who knew they had diabetes, only thirty six point three percent reached the glycaemic target.

11
00:01:11,763 --> 00:01:17,745
Only seven point seven percent reached the targets for blood sugar, blood pressure and LDL cholesterol together.

12
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:21,122
Diet is a large part of the picture.

13
00:01:21,522 --> 00:01:26,280
Carbohydrates supply sixty two point three percent of total daily energy across India.

14
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:36,168
In the same survey's 2025 analysis in Nature Medicine, replacing five percent of energy from carbohydrate with protein was associated with lower risk.

15
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:42,107
Swapping refined cereals for whole wheat or millet, without eating less carbohydrate, was not.

16
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:49,388
And care usually sees the result weeks later, at the next clinic visit, as an HbA1c number.

17
00:01:49,788 --> 00:01:56,245
The spike after Tuesday's lunch, or the low at three in the morning, has already happened, and nobody saw it coming.

18
00:01:57,745 --> 00:01:59,680
So this project asks one question.

19
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:09,757
Can a doctor see the next two hours of a patient's glucose, get a warning with reasons before a spike or a low, and test a lifestyle change on that patient's own twin before advising it?

20
00:02:11,257 --> 00:02:13,510
The challenge brief asks for exactly this.

21
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:19,985
Two data streams fused into one model: here, a FHIR health record and a CGM plus wearable stream.

22
00:02:20,385 --> 00:02:26,500
An adverse event predicted ahead of time: here, glucose spikes and lows, each warned with its reasons.

23
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,639
A dashboard for the clinician: here, a working doctor's screen.

24
00:02:31,039 --> 00:02:37,166
And sandbox data only: every patient you will see is either synthetic, or from an open research dataset.

25
00:02:38,666 --> 00:02:40,841
Chapter two. What GlucoTwin is.

26
00:02:42,641 --> 00:02:48,261
GlucoTwin is a personal, physiological digital twin for adults with type 2 diabetes or prediabetes.

27
00:02:48,661 --> 00:02:49,938
It joins three sources.

28
00:02:50,338 --> 00:02:53,078
The health record, which says who this person is.

29
00:02:53,478 --> 00:02:57,842
The continuous glucose monitor, which says what their glucose is doing right now.

30
00:02:58,242 --> 00:03:05,099
And wearable signals, like sleep, heart rate variability and walks, which say how their body is likely to respond today.

31
00:03:06,599 --> 00:03:08,733
From these, the twin does three jobs.

32
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:15,820
First, it forecasts glucose thirty, sixty and one hundred and twenty minutes ahead, with an uncertainty band.

33
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:22,042
Second, it raises spike and low alerts before they happen, and attaches the reasons it sees on the curve.

34
00:03:22,442 --> 00:03:29,418
Third, it compares seven lifestyle plans on that person's own twin, to show which change helps this patient, and which does not.

35
00:03:30,918 --> 00:03:35,681
The user is a diabetologist or primary care doctor, reviewing patients between visits.

36
00:03:36,081 --> 00:03:41,443
It is decision support for the doctor: it never doses a medicine, and never makes a diagnosis.

37
00:03:42,943 --> 00:03:44,605
Chapter three. The data.

38
00:03:46,405 --> 00:03:47,879
The twin reads two streams.

39
00:03:48,279 --> 00:04:00,571
Stream one is the health record, written as FHIR R4 resources: age and sex, the diagnosis, lab observations such as HbA1c, fasting glucose and fasting insulin, and the medication list.

40
00:04:00,971 --> 00:04:05,717
FHIR R4 is also the format India's ABDM uses to exchange health records.

41
00:04:06,117 --> 00:04:15,586
Stream two is the sensor stream: CGM every five minutes, heart rate, sleep and heart rate variability, steps, and the meals and walks the patient logs.

42
00:04:15,986 --> 00:04:21,903
Meal portions use the Indian Food Composition Tables, 2017, for verified dishes.

43
00:04:23,403 --> 00:04:28,519
In the dashboard, the Two-Stream Inspector shows exactly this, for one patient at one minute.

44
00:04:28,919 --> 00:04:33,222
On the left, every FHIR resource from the record, and what the twin does with it.

45
00:04:33,622 --> 00:04:37,470
On the right, the wearable packet at this minute, and the fitted knobs.

46
00:04:37,870 --> 00:04:45,733
Rows that are only displayed say so: triglycerides, HDL and LDL are listed but not used, and heart rate is shown only.

47
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:50,525
Not every signal changes the model, and the build says which.

48
00:04:50,925 --> 00:04:58,092
CGM, logged meals, logged walks, sleep duration and efficiency, and heart rate variability all feed the twin.

49
00:04:58,492 --> 00:05:03,306
So do the diagnosis, the labs, BMI, sex and the medication list.

50
00:05:03,706 --> 00:05:12,277
Heart rate, step counts and sleep stages are displayed but not modelled, and on the simulated ward, age acts only through an approximate subgroup label.

51
00:05:13,777 --> 00:05:15,553
Two datasets drive the results.

52
00:05:15,953 --> 00:05:23,058
The first is real: CGMacros, an open PhysioNet dataset of forty five adults in Santa Barbara, California.

53
00:05:23,458 --> 00:05:28,309
Fourteen have type 2 diabetes, sixteen have prediabetes, and fifteen are healthy.

54
00:05:28,709 --> 00:05:33,301
They wore two CGMs and a smartwatch, and logged meals with their macronutrients.

55
00:05:33,701 --> 00:05:39,683
It is licensed Creative Commons, non-commercial, share-alike, and the raw files are not in the repository.

56
00:05:41,183 --> 00:05:47,403
The second is a simulated ward of eleven synthetic Indian patients, generated from a fixed seed, forty two.

57
00:05:47,803 --> 00:05:51,939
Eight have type 2 diabetes, two have prediabetes, and one is healthy.

58
00:05:52,339 --> 00:05:59,459
Its generator deliberately uses different equations from the twin: a different time-of-day effect, walk carry-over and drug effect.

59
00:05:59,859 --> 00:06:02,937
So the twin has to learn a body it was not written to match.

60
00:06:03,337 --> 00:06:08,516
A second, matched generator that shares the twin's equations is reported only as an upper bound.

61
00:06:10,016 --> 00:06:12,282
Every result uses a strict split in time.

62
00:06:12,682 --> 00:06:20,723
On the simulated ward, days one to five fit each twin, days six to nine choose thresholds and settings, and days ten to twenty one are the test.

63
00:06:21,123 --> 00:06:27,296
On CGMacros, days one to four fit, days five and six validate, and day seven onward is the test.

64
00:06:27,696 --> 00:06:29,318
Test days never choose anything.

65
00:06:30,818 --> 00:06:33,033
No real patient record appears anywhere.

66
00:06:33,433 --> 00:06:44,667
The simulated people are fictional, CGMacros is open and de-identified with shifted dates, and the dashboard simulates per-stream consent, in the spirit of India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act.

67
00:06:46,167 --> 00:06:48,005
Chapter four. Architecture.

68
00:06:49,805 --> 00:06:53,847
Here is the architecture, as built. Data flows from left to right.

69
00:06:54,247 --> 00:07:00,603
On the left are the inputs: CGM, wearable signals, the FHIR record, and logged meals and walks.

70
00:07:01,003 --> 00:07:08,888
First, stream checks put every reading on a five-minute grid, and flag sensor gaps, compression lows, sensor lag and unit mix-ups.

71
00:07:09,288 --> 00:07:14,646
A compression low is a false low from lying on the sensor at night; those minutes are kept out of the twin.

72
00:07:15,046 --> 00:07:21,556
Next is the consent gate. Each stream can be withdrawn on its own, and the twin's uncertainty band widens when it is.

73
00:07:21,956 --> 00:07:30,663
At the centre is the twin core: a physiological model with five personal knobs, fitted to each patient, which produces the two-hour forecast and its conformal band.

74
00:07:31,063 --> 00:07:37,205
Its outputs feed two engines. The alert engine turns forecast risk into Urgent or Watch alerts with reasons.

75
00:07:37,605 --> 00:07:42,263
The plan engine simulates seven lifestyle plans on the same twin, and ranks them.

76
00:07:42,663 --> 00:07:45,669
Everything ends on one screen: the doctor's dashboard.

77
00:07:46,069 --> 00:07:56,069
On the right is the offline column: a Python evaluator that scores the model on the real CGMacros files, learns a ridge prior from the labs, and tests a LightGBM residual.

78
00:07:56,469 --> 00:08:01,068
None of the offline pieces are added to the live forecast. They are there to measure it.

79
00:08:02,568 --> 00:08:09,398
This is the same architecture as published in the repository, and in the submission as a PDF, with notes on what is not built.

80
00:08:10,898 --> 00:08:16,344
The app is TypeScript, with React 19 and TanStack Start, and Recharts for the charts.

81
00:08:16,744 --> 00:08:22,374
The offline evaluator is Python, with numpy, pandas, scikit-learn and LightGBM.

82
00:08:22,774 --> 00:08:30,943
A parity check runs fifteen shared functions on twenty cases each, and requires the Python and TypeScript versions to agree within one part in a million.

83
00:08:32,443 --> 00:08:39,995
To keep the dashboard fast, the fitted simulated ward ships as a snapshot file, which the browser rebuilds in about a quarter of a second.

84
00:08:40,395 --> 00:08:44,814
The test suite refits the ward, and fails if that snapshot no longer matches.

85
00:08:45,214 --> 00:08:52,105
Type checking, three hundred and twenty tests, the parity check and the production build run on every push, in GitHub Actions.

86
00:08:53,605 --> 00:08:55,491
Chapter five. The approach.

87
00:08:57,291 --> 00:09:00,208
Why a physiological model, and not a black box?

88
00:09:00,608 --> 00:09:07,016
Because a doctor needs to see why the forecast moves: a meal, a walk, the dawn rise, last night's sleep.

89
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:14,514
And because a mechanistic twin can answer what-if questions, like moving lunch an hour earlier, that a pattern-matcher has never seen.

90
00:09:16,014 --> 00:09:18,732
Each logged meal adds a rise shaped like a triangle.

91
00:09:19,132 --> 00:09:24,412
It peaks around forty five minutes, later for a meal with more fibre, and fades by three hours.

92
00:09:24,812 --> 00:09:29,200
Its height is the person's carb sensitivity, times the grams of carbohydrate.

93
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:35,454
A meal clock makes the same meal rise more in the evening and at dawn, and big meals rise more than in proportion.

94
00:09:35,854 --> 00:09:42,115
On CGMacros, the validation days switched those clock factors off, because they did not help on real data.

95
00:09:43,615 --> 00:09:50,051
A walk lowers glucose during and just after it, and raises insulin sensitivity for twelve to thirty hours afterwards.

96
00:09:50,451 --> 00:09:52,528
A dawn term adds the early-morning rise.

97
00:09:52,928 --> 00:10:03,004
And last night's sleep debt, poor sleep efficiency, and a drop in heart rate variability against the person's own baseline, raise carb sensitivity and the dawn rise for the whole day.

98
00:10:03,404 --> 00:10:07,181
Half of the night before still counts, because sleep debt accumulates.

99
00:10:08,681 --> 00:10:17,114
Every twin has five personal knobs: carb sensitivity, activity effect, fasting baseline, dawn amplitude, and the meal time constant.

100
00:10:17,514 --> 00:10:19,833
Days one to five fit them from the CGM.

101
00:10:21,333 --> 00:10:25,992
But on day one there is no CGM history, so the twin starts from the health record.

102
00:10:26,392 --> 00:10:34,938
On the simulated ward, a record formula maps HbA1c, fasting glucose, insulin resistance, BMI and sex to a starting twin.

103
00:10:35,338 --> 00:10:46,023
An approximate Indian subgroup label, SIDD, IROD, CIRDD or MARD, adds a shift, and it is clearly marked as an approximation of the published method.

104
00:10:46,423 --> 00:10:52,345
On CGMacros, the prior is a leave-one-out ridge regression on the labs, BMI, age and sex.

105
00:10:53,845 --> 00:10:59,177
The personal fit is then shrunk toward that prior, by an amount chosen per patient on validation days.

106
00:10:59,577 --> 00:11:00,902
Two safety floors follow.

107
00:11:01,302 --> 00:11:06,639
If the personal twin does not beat its prior by more than one standard error, the twin keeps the prior.

108
00:11:07,039 --> 00:11:11,923
And if the record-based twin does not beat the plain group average, it falls back to the group.

109
00:11:13,423 --> 00:11:18,872
The forecast adds the meal, walk and baseline curves to the current residual, which decays over time.

110
00:11:19,272 --> 00:11:21,726
Around it sits a ten-to-ninety percent band.

111
00:11:22,126 --> 00:11:30,417
It is split conformal: the band's edges are order statistics of real errors on validation days, so it reflects how wrong the twin has actually been.

112
00:11:31,917 --> 00:11:40,091
To warn early, the twin also anticipates the person's habitual next meal, learned from the fitting days: its usual time, and its usual size.

113
00:11:40,491 --> 00:11:42,044
It never reads future logs.

114
00:11:42,444 --> 00:11:48,494
That is why a spike warning can arrive before the patient has even started eating, like this one on Ananya's list.

115
00:11:49,994 --> 00:11:59,886
Spike risk is the chance that the forecast peak goes above one eighty, or rises fifty above now, in the next two hours. Low risk is the chance it falls under seventy within the hour.

116
00:12:00,286 --> 00:12:10,114
Those limits follow the international consensus on time in range, Battelino and colleagues, Diabetes Care 2019, which the ADA Standards of Care use.

117
00:12:10,514 --> 00:12:15,815
Alerts use hysteresis, a suppression window and a daily cap, so the doctor is not flooded.

118
00:12:16,215 --> 00:12:25,313
An alert is Urgent for a low, a peak above two fifty, or an hour above one eighty. Otherwise it is Watch, and a Watch is upgraded in place if the curve worsens.

119
00:12:26,813 --> 00:12:28,486
The medication list matters too.

120
00:12:28,886 --> 00:12:38,777
When the record lists a sulfonylurea, the twin expects meal rises to shrink, and glucose to fall during long gaps after a meal. A low guard then projects the rest of that fall.

121
00:12:39,177 --> 00:12:45,236
Those guard settings were chosen on a separate, independent run of the ward, so the scored days never tuned them.

122
00:12:46,736 --> 00:12:48,846
That principle runs through the whole project.

123
00:12:49,246 --> 00:12:55,343
Every threshold and setting is chosen on validation days or on independent data, never on the days that are scored.

124
00:12:55,743 --> 00:13:02,451
On CGMacros, the low-alert rule is chosen leave-one-participant-out, so no participant's own days pick their rule.

125
00:13:02,851 --> 00:13:07,798
And where an earlier version did look at test data, the change log says so in plain words.

126
00:13:09,298 --> 00:13:11,256
Chapter six. The dashboard.

127
00:13:13,056 --> 00:13:16,865
This is the doctor's dashboard, running locally on the simulated ward.

128
00:13:17,265 --> 00:13:25,614
The cohort screen ranks all eleven patients by who needs a look now, with current glucose, a sparkline, time in range, and any active alert.

129
00:13:26,014 --> 00:13:32,440
Each row also carries the approximate subgroup label, and flags like the size of the dawn rise, or a hidden snack.

130
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:38,318
The chips across the top jump to teaching moments, and Replay plays the ward forward, minute by minute.

131
00:13:39,818 --> 00:13:41,072
Opening Ananya's twin.

132
00:13:41,472 --> 00:13:48,725
The record card shows her labs: HbA1c eight point one percent, BMI twenty nine point four, and her metformin.

133
00:13:49,125 --> 00:13:56,521
The solid line is her last six hours of CGM. The dashed line is the two-hour forecast, and the shaded wash is the conformal band.

134
00:13:56,921 --> 00:14:02,756
Amber dots are meals. Her lunch is on the curve, and the forecast already rises well past one eighty.

135
00:14:03,156 --> 00:14:10,075
Below the chart are her fitted knobs. Her carb sensitivity is fourteen point two, against a type 2 group average of twelve.

136
00:14:11,575 --> 00:14:12,800
Now the consent gate.

137
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:17,408
Withdrawing the health record removes her record prior, and her medication list.

138
00:14:17,808 --> 00:14:25,780
The twin keeps her personal CGM fit, shrunk toward the type 2 group instead, and the band widens, here by a factor of one point one two.

139
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:29,731
Alerts, plans and the ward ranking all follow the consented twin.

140
00:14:31,231 --> 00:14:34,441
On the alerts screen, every alert carries its reasons.

141
00:14:34,841 --> 00:14:42,319
This Urgent spike lines up with the sixty seven grams of carbohydrate she has just eaten, and her glucose against her fasting baseline.

142
00:14:42,719 --> 00:14:50,754
The one below it fired at eleven twenty five, seventy five minutes before her usual lunch, from the habit prior, before anything was logged.

143
00:14:51,154 --> 00:14:56,496
The doctor can acknowledge an alert or mark it a false alarm, and only Urgent alerts interrupt.

144
00:14:57,996 --> 00:15:02,881
This is Divya, a simulated patient whose record adds a sulfonylurea from day sixteen.

145
00:15:03,281 --> 00:15:14,188
Her low alert leads with a Medicine reason: the drug is on the record, it is six hours since her last logged meal, and the guard expects up to thirty five more milligrams per decilitre of fall within the hour.

146
00:15:14,588 --> 00:15:17,156
It came true, with only five minutes of lead.

147
00:15:17,556 --> 00:15:23,144
And to be honest about it: on the scored days, the blind guard catches just one of her ten drug lows.

148
00:15:24,644 --> 00:15:27,178
The what-if screen is where a twin earns its name.

149
00:15:27,578 --> 00:15:43,181
Seven plans are applied to the patient's highest-risk meal: the meal as logged, a fifteen-minute walk after it, a five percent carb cut made up with dal and paneer, eating sixty minutes earlier, jowar roti at the same carbohydrate, splitting the meal, and one fasting day a week.

150
00:15:43,581 --> 00:15:48,728
Each is simulated on her own twin over days ten to sixteen, and ranked by net glycaemic gain.

151
00:15:49,128 --> 00:15:54,875
For Ananya, the fasting day and the walk come out on top, each adding about two points of time in range.

152
00:15:56,375 --> 00:15:59,021
Each plan carries a reason specific to this patient.

153
00:15:59,421 --> 00:16:07,001
Her carb sensitivity is one point one eight times the type 2 average, so a five percent carb cut moves her twin more than an average one.

154
00:16:07,401 --> 00:16:15,766
Jowar roti is listed honestly: the Nature Medicine analysis found no risk drop for that swap, so it is shown as a preference, not a benefit.

155
00:16:16,166 --> 00:16:25,711
In the ranking, a minute under seventy counts as three minutes above one eighty, or six on a glucose-lowering drug, and the fasting day always warns to review those medicines first.

156
00:16:27,211 --> 00:16:29,876
Many patients read Hindi more easily than English.

157
00:16:30,276 --> 00:16:35,612
One switch translates the plan table, each plan's patient-specific reason, and the printed summary.

158
00:16:37,112 --> 00:16:46,317
Below the plans, the doctor can add a verified Indian food portion, a walk, or change last night's sleep, and watch the simulated curve move against the logged one.

159
00:16:46,717 --> 00:16:51,444
It is labelled simulation, not advice, and nothing on this screen changes a dose.

160
00:16:52,944 --> 00:16:56,535
The clinical summary button prints a one-page pre-visit sheet for the chart.

161
00:16:56,935 --> 00:17:05,302
It holds the last seven days, the twin's parameters, the top-ranked plan with its simulated change, and last week's Urgent alerts with their outcomes.

162
00:17:06,802 --> 00:17:14,333
The split view breaks the current reading into its pieces: the circadian baseline, any active meal or walk, and an unexplained residual.

163
00:17:14,733 --> 00:17:23,630
Here, a snack Ananya never logged. The twin cannot see it, so it sits inside the residual, and the screen can replay the forecast as if it had been logged.

164
00:17:25,130 --> 00:17:32,361
And here, a sensor gap. There is no glucose to split, so the forecast and its alerts stay off, rather than guessing.

165
00:17:33,861 --> 00:17:39,777
The night view reviews each morning: the dawn rise from four to eight, nights with lows, and short sleep.

166
00:17:40,177 --> 00:17:46,359
Fatima's mornings rise by a median of twenty two points, and the morning after her short night rose by thirty five.

167
00:17:47,859 --> 00:17:54,931
Finally, one switch moves the dashboard onto real data: three held-out CGMacros participants, one from each group.

168
00:17:55,331 --> 00:18:01,522
The same twin, alerts and plans run on their real glucose, meals and watch activity, with dates shifted.

169
00:18:01,922 --> 00:18:04,756
The evaluation that follows covers all forty five.

170
00:18:06,256 --> 00:18:08,023
Chapter seven. Results.

171
00:18:09,823 --> 00:18:13,589
First, forecasting on the forty five real CGMacros adults.

172
00:18:13,989 --> 00:18:27,007
Persistence, the baseline that assumes glucose stays where it is, has errors of nineteen point six, twenty eight point nine and thirty eight point nine milligrams per decilitre at thirty, sixty and one hundred and twenty minutes.

173
00:18:27,407 --> 00:18:32,285
The twin's errors are eighteen point five, twenty six point two and thirty one point eight.

174
00:18:32,685 --> 00:18:40,444
That is eighteen point three percent lower at two hours, with a participant bootstrap interval of fourteen point nine to twenty one point four percent.

175
00:18:40,844 --> 00:18:45,269
It beats persistence at every horizon, by the narrowest margin at thirty minutes.

176
00:18:46,769 --> 00:18:53,892
Ninety nine point eight percent of thirty-minute forecasts fall in Clarke error grid zones A and B, the clinically acceptable zones.

177
00:18:54,292 --> 00:19:01,556
And the ten-to-ninety percent band covers between seventy nine and eighty one percent of real outcomes, against an eighty percent target.

178
00:19:03,056 --> 00:19:06,266
Does the health record help? On real data, yes.

179
00:19:06,666 --> 00:19:09,893
With no record, sixty-minute error is thirty point four.

180
00:19:10,293 --> 00:19:19,021
With a prior from the labs, BMI, age and sex alone, before any CGM fitting, it drops to twenty six point eight: twelve percent lower.

181
00:19:19,421 --> 00:19:22,621
With personal calibration on top, twenty six point four.

182
00:19:24,121 --> 00:19:29,665
On the simulated stress test, the twin warns a median of one hundred and five minutes before a spike.

183
00:19:30,065 --> 00:19:37,043
One hundred and nine of two hundred and fifty four spikes are warned at least an hour ahead, and thirty three a full two hours ahead.

184
00:19:37,443 --> 00:19:45,564
Sixty one percent of spikes are warned, and sixty seven percent of spike alerts come true, with zero point seven five false alerts per patient-day.

185
00:19:45,964 --> 00:19:53,656
Urgent alerts reach one hundred and twenty five of one hundred and eighty five severe events, and eighty three point five percent of them are right.

186
00:19:55,156 --> 00:19:56,857
Is that better than a simple alarm?

187
00:19:57,257 --> 00:20:07,313
On the same spikes with the same scoring, a sixty-minute CGM trend alarm reaches the same F1 score: zero point six three seven, against the twin's zero point six three eight.

188
00:20:07,713 --> 00:20:16,991
But it warns a median of twenty five minutes ahead instead of one hundred and five, and raises two point one one false alarms per patient-day, nearly three times as many.

189
00:20:18,491 --> 00:20:27,854
As a ranking, the twin's spike risk has an area under the ROC curve of zero point nine four seven on the simulated ward, and zero point six eight five on real data.

190
00:20:28,254 --> 00:20:32,175
For lows, zero point eight five three and zero point six six eight.

191
00:20:32,575 --> 00:20:38,604
On real data the risk number is not a calibrated probability, and the documentation says to read it as a ranking.

192
00:20:40,104 --> 00:20:42,777
Lows are the hardest part, because they are rare.

193
00:20:43,177 --> 00:20:48,830
On real data, the twin warns seven of seventeen lows, and seven of its eighteen low alerts come true.

194
00:20:49,230 --> 00:20:53,948
On the simulated ward it warns six of sixteen, and all six alerts are right.

195
00:20:54,348 --> 00:20:57,698
Both are below our targets, and both are reported as they are.

196
00:20:59,198 --> 00:21:00,699
Which streams earn their place?

197
00:21:01,099 --> 00:21:06,589
Removing meals raises simulated sixty-minute error from twenty two point nine to twenty six point four.

198
00:21:06,989 --> 00:21:14,416
Activity matters most after a walk: within six hours of one, error is fifteen point eight with it, and sixteen point four without.

199
00:21:14,816 --> 00:21:20,649
Sleep and heart rate variability help after a short or stressed night, and add almost nothing on an average day.

200
00:21:21,049 --> 00:21:27,723
And without the record and its medicine term, pooled error barely moves, but warned lows fall from six of sixteen to three.

201
00:21:29,223 --> 00:21:38,590
The clearest sign that the twin is personal: the same plan, splitting lunch, raises time in range for Vikram by three point six points, and for Kavita by two point nine.

202
00:21:38,990 --> 00:21:43,112
It lowers it for Leela by five point four, and for Fatima by four point one.

203
00:21:43,512 --> 00:21:48,980
It helps four of the eight type 2 twins, and hurts four. A one-size rule would get half of them wrong.

204
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:52,072
Chapter eight. Scale.

205
00:21:53,872 --> 00:21:55,158
The twin is cheap to run.

206
00:21:55,558 --> 00:22:04,522
One update, a two-hour forecast and a spike risk take sixteen point three microseconds per reading on one laptop thread: about sixty one thousand readings a second.

207
00:22:04,922 --> 00:22:08,103
Each patient's twin state is about one point seven kilobytes.

208
00:22:09,603 --> 00:22:18,138
A patient produces two hundred and eighty eight CGM readings a day, so a month of model compute is about zero point one four CPU-seconds per patient.

209
00:22:18,538 --> 00:22:27,293
At a million patients, that is about thirty nine CPU-hours a month, three thousand three hundred readings a second, and one point seven gigabytes of state.

210
00:22:27,693 --> 00:22:31,170
The sensors and hosting, not the model, set the cost.

211
00:22:32,670 --> 00:22:39,768
Each update takes constant time and touches only that patient's state, so workers can be stateless, and sharded by patient ID.

212
00:22:40,168 --> 00:22:43,290
Ranking all seven plans takes about eleven milliseconds.

213
00:22:43,690 --> 00:22:51,271
And because the twin already reads FHIR R4, it is ready for ABDM record exchange; that import is on the roadmap, not built yet.

214
00:22:52,771 --> 00:22:58,288
Scale in India also means cost to the patient, and not everyone can keep buying CGM sensors.

215
00:22:58,688 --> 00:23:07,130
On the simulated ward, eight fingersticks a day rebuild the glucose curve with about twenty milligrams per decilitre of error, and six a day with twenty one.

216
00:23:07,530 --> 00:23:12,640
That is interpolation error, not forecast error, but it shows a path for step-down care.

217
00:23:14,140 --> 00:23:16,352
Chapter nine. Real-world impact.

218
00:23:18,152 --> 00:23:24,392
In an Indian OPD, a diabetologist may have a few minutes per patient. The twin adds five things to those minutes.

219
00:23:24,792 --> 00:23:30,119
A rice-heavy lunch, and whether dal and paneer, or jowar roti, would help this particular person.

220
00:23:30,519 --> 00:23:34,039
A patient starting a sulfonylurea who wants to fast once a week.

221
00:23:34,439 --> 00:23:38,496
A sharp night-time low that may be a compression artefact rather than a real low.

222
00:23:38,896 --> 00:23:41,732
A patient stepping down from CGM to fingersticks.

223
00:23:42,132 --> 00:23:43,611
And a patient sheet in Hindi.

224
00:23:45,111 --> 00:23:46,869
The impact is a change in timing.

225
00:23:47,269 --> 00:23:57,039
Instead of reading last month's HbA1c, the doctor sees the next two hours, gets warnings with reasons, and tests a plan on this patient's own twin before advising it.

226
00:23:58,539 --> 00:24:00,655
The next step is a supervised pilot.

227
00:24:01,055 --> 00:24:09,352
One diabetes clinic, thirty consenting adults with type 2 diabetes on CGM for twelve weeks, with a doctor reviewing every forecast and alert.

228
00:24:09,752 --> 00:24:16,492
We would judge it on time in range, spikes warned an hour ahead, false alerts below one per patient-day, and review time.

229
00:24:17,992 --> 00:24:27,161
Alongside the pilot, the roadmap adds consented FHIR import through ABDM, learned medicine effects for lows, and risk calibrated on real Indian CGM data.

230
00:24:28,661 --> 00:24:30,828
Chapter ten. Limits and safety.

231
00:24:32,628 --> 00:24:35,649
This is a research prototype, not a medical device.

232
00:24:36,049 --> 00:24:42,113
CGMacros is forty five adults in the United States, so results may not transfer to Indian patients.

233
00:24:42,513 --> 00:24:46,708
The simulated ward's sleep, walk and drug effects are modelling assumptions.

234
00:24:47,108 --> 00:24:51,016
Low warnings are still weak, and real-data risk is not yet calibrated.

235
00:24:51,416 --> 00:24:58,426
The subgroup rule is an approximation of the published INSPIRED method. And the plan comparison is a simulation, not advice.

236
00:24:59,926 --> 00:25:01,064
Safety is built in.

237
00:25:01,464 --> 00:25:07,596
No dosing and no diagnosis, anywhere. Every alert carries its reasons. Consent is per stream.

238
00:25:07,996 --> 00:25:11,963
The printed note can only repeat numbers that come from the patient's own data.

239
00:25:12,364 --> 00:25:19,786
And a model card in the repository lists the intended use, the population, the equations, every metric, and the known limits.

240
00:25:21,286 --> 00:25:25,009
GlucoTwin: a digital twin that shows the doctor the next two hours.

241
00:25:25,409 --> 00:25:33,480
Built by Nikhil Chawla, team GlucoTwin_RIMT-University, for the Happiest Health Digital Twin Challenge 2026.

242
00:25:33,880 --> 00:25:39,637
The code, the model card, and every number in this video are in the repository. Thank you for watching.
